Core Reasoning

I am investing again in Alphabet because I believe the company is beginning to expose a structure that I predicted more generally before I had an investment framework: AI will become increasingly interconnected rather than remain a collection of isolated models, applications and devices.

My current thesis is more specific.

I do not think the most important long-term AI asset will necessarily be the single model that produces the best answer in isolation. As model capability becomes cheaper and more abundant, the bottleneck shifts toward the system surrounding the model:

context,

persistent state,

identity,

permissions,

memory,

routing,

agent discovery,

coordination,

execution,

feedback from the real world.

This is consistent with the broader investment framework I have already documented: AI does not remove scarcity; it moves scarcity. When generation becomes abundant, the value of ordering, integration and execution rises.

Alphabet is unusually interesting under this framework because it already owns a large number of the nodes required to build an interconnected intelligence system:

