10. Why I Am Not Making This a 10% Position

The thesis is strong enough for me to allocate capital.

It is not strong enough for me to ignore price and capital intensity.

At the reference price of approximately USD 344.98, Alphabet's market capitalization is around USD 4.2 trillion.

The stock is therefore already priced as one of the most valuable businesses ever created.

A correct technological thesis does not automatically imply an attractive stock return.

Alphabet can execute technically and still disappoint shareholders if:

the market already prices most of the upside,

AI infrastructure becomes structurally too capital intensive,

competition compresses returns,

Search economics weaken,

connected intelligence does not create strong willingness to pay,

regulation restricts the most valuable integrations,

interoperability lowers rather than increases switching costs.

Capital expenditure is the most important financial counterweight to my thesis.

Alphabet entered 2026 planning USD 175–185 billion of CapEx and subsequently raised its infrastructure investment expectations further during the year. Even before the latest increase, management explicitly warned that higher technical-infrastructure investment would increase depreciation and data-center operating costs.

The company is building the infrastructure required for the upside case, but shareholders only benefit if the future cash flows produced by that infrastructure exceed the capital required to build and continuously replace it.

That is not yet fully proven.

Therefore my allocation is 5%, not 10%.

The 5% weight means:

I have enough conviction to make Alphabet a meaningful position, but not enough evidence to treat the outcome as close to certain.

