15. Bear, Base and Bull Cases

Bear Case — 25%

My architectural prediction is partly correct, but the economics are poor.

Google connects more products, devices and agents, but users do not pay enough for the additional utility. Search monetization weakens. Open protocols commoditize interoperability. Competitors own important user interfaces. Regulation limits context integration. Capital expenditure remains extremely high and normalized free cash flow disappoints.

In this scenario, I may have correctly predicted the direction of technology while making a poor investment because value capture was weaker than expected.

That distinction is important for the postmortem.

Base Case — 50%

Alphabet remains one of several dominant global AI platforms.

Gemini is competitive enough; it does not need permanent model supremacy.

Search remains economically resilient. Google Cloud keeps scaling. Personal context and cross-device continuity become materially useful. A2A and agent infrastructure become meaningful parts of enterprise deployment. Capital intensity remains high but eventually produces sufficient utilization and cash flow.

Alphabet's advantage comes from controlling a large fraction of the context, infrastructure, distribution and action surfaces required by interconnected AI.

Bull Case — 25%

Alphabet successfully converts its collection of products into a coherent intelligence graph.

Gemini becomes persistent across digital surfaces and devices. The combination of Search, Gmail, Maps, YouTube, Chrome, Android and Workspace creates superior authorized context. A2A and Agent Registry make Google Cloud an important control plane for large agent ecosystems. Watches and glasses create persistent and environmental context. Physical AI becomes a significant additional platform.

In this outcome, the value of Alphabet is no longer well described as the sum of Search + YouTube + Cloud + Android + devices.

The edges between those businesses become a major source of value.

That is the nonlinear upside case.

16. Final Investment Thesis

I am not investing in Alphabet because I predict that Gemini will permanently be the world's best language model.

I am investing because I expect intelligence itself to become increasingly persistent, interconnected and embedded across digital and physical environments.

As that happens, the scarce layer moves away from isolated model output and toward:

context,

state,

identity,

permissions,

discovery,

coordination,

execution,

feedback.

Alphabet owns an unusually large fraction of the nodes necessary to build this layer.

Search, Gmail, Maps, YouTube, Photos, Chrome and Workspace can supply context.

Google Account and Android can supply identity and distribution.

Gemini can supply reasoning.

A2A and Agent Registry can supply interoperability, discovery and governance.

Google Cloud and TPUs can supply compute and deployment infrastructure.

Phones, watches, cars and glasses can turn intelligence into a persistent interface across different parts of daily life.

Gemini Robotics can eventually extend the same architecture from digital action into physical action.

The individual products are not the thesis.

The graph between them is the thesis.

The real-option component is particularly important. Several of the most interesting assets — watches, intelligent glasses, agent-control infrastructure and robotics — do not need to become enormous standalone businesses to create value. They can matter because they add new high-value edges to an already enormous network.

This is the specific pattern I want to recognize before it becomes completely obvious in consolidated financial statements:

products that are currently too small to matter financially, but too strategically coherent and increasingly interconnected to dismiss as isolated experiments.

Alphabet is not a hidden company and its ecosystem is not unknown. My differentiated bet is narrower: the company may be in the early stages of transforming its existing ecosystem into a persistent context–state–action infrastructure for AI.

At approximately USD 4.2 trillion of market value, the price already assumes extraordinary future success. The position therefore remains deliberately limited to 5% of the virtual portfolio.

The thesis is strong.

The valuation and capital-intensity uncertainty are also real.

